WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
Every exported symbol can become part of a compatibility and maintenance surface.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:14.040
If internal functions, types, or data are visible, downstream code may start using them even when they were never intended as public ABI.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:21.340
Hidden symbols reduce accidental coupling, symbol collisions, and reliance on implementation details.

4
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.880
A smaller export surface is easier to review, test, and preserve across releases.

5
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.040
Versioning makes compatibility expectations visible.

6
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:39.180
API and ABI versions, symbol versions, versioned structs, and release notes help callers understand what changed and what remains supported.

7
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:47.680
Versioning should be paired with policy: which changes are backward compatible, which require rebuilding clients, and which are unsupported internal details.

8
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.300
Without policy, a version number is only decoration.

9
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.460
Dependencies can alter ABI behavior even when public source headers look similar.

10
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:07.360
Compiler versions, standard library versions, runtime library choices, build flags, structure packing, and dependency updates may affect binary compatibility.

11
00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:14.500
Release review should inspect exported symbols, binary metadata, dependency changes, and compatibility notes.

12
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:20.360
ABI changes should be intentional, documented, and approved before they reach users.

13
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:26.540
A release process that ignores binary artifacts can miss exactly the changes that older callers depend on.

14
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:36.500
A practical release checklist should compare exported symbols against a known baseline, record dependency updates, and call out toolchain or runtime changes.

15
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:46.120
When a symbol is removed, renamed, or newly exposed, reviewers should decide whether that change is compatible, accidental, or a deliberate breaking release.

16
00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:52.300
The value is not bureaucracy; it is making binary risk visible before customers discover it.
